WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.281
Razor Pages are page-focused ASP.NET Core
applications.

2
00:00:05.361 --> 00:00:11.020
That page-centered design can make
features easier to reason about,

3
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:14.418
but it does not make security automatic.

4
00:00:14.498 --> 00:00:19.864
A single page can accept form input, bind
values into a page model,

5
00:00:19.944 --> 00:00:25.701
change records, return user-specific data,
write logs, set cookies,

6
00:00:25.781 --> 00:00:28.708
and depend on deployment settings.

7
00:00:28.788 --> 00:00:34.447
Each of those behaviors is part of the
application security surface.

8
00:00:34.527 --> 00:00:41.064
Security decisions in Razor Pages often
appear close to everyday feature code.

9
00:00:41.144 --> 00:00:47.096
They show up in page models, handler
methods, route conventions, forms,

10
00:00:47.176 --> 00:00:53.811
validation attributes, Razor output,
authorization configuration, cookies,

11
00:00:53.891 --> 00:01:00.819
upload handling, error behavior, logging
choices, and production configuration.

12
00:01:00.899 --> 00:01:07.436
A secure application does not hide those
decisions in accidental conventions.

13
00:01:07.516 --> 00:01:12.102
It makes them easy for developers and
reviewers to find.

14
00:01:12.182 --> 00:01:16.280
Authentication is only one piece of the
baseline.

15
00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:22.897
Knowing who made a request does not answer
whether that identity may view a page,

16
00:01:22.977 --> 00:01:29.026
change a record, submit a form, download
content, update configuration,

17
00:01:29.106 --> 00:01:32.424
or perform an administrative workflow.

18
00:01:32.504 --> 00:01:36.797
Razor Pages applications also need
authorization,

19
00:01:36.877 --> 00:01:43.512
request intent validation, server-side
input validation, safe model binding,

20
00:01:43.592 --> 00:01:49.251
output encoding, careful state handling,
and production controls.

21
00:01:49.331 --> 00:01:54.795
A strong Razor Pages design makes access
rules, input boundaries,

22
00:01:54.875 --> 00:01:59.461
state-changing actions, and output
behavior visible.

23
00:01:59.541 --> 00:02:04.224
Reviewers should be able to answer
practical questions:

24
00:02:04.304 --> 00:02:08.890
who can reach this page, what handlers can
change state,

25
00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:13.751
what data is bound from the request, what
validation runs,

26
00:02:13.831 --> 00:02:19.393
what data is displayed, and what
operational signals are recorded.

27
00:02:19.473 --> 00:02:23.278
The goal is not to make every page
complicated.

28
00:02:23.358 --> 00:02:27.358
The goal is to make important behavior
explicit.

29
00:02:27.438 --> 00:02:33.976
A clean Razor Pages baseline lets teams
keep the page model readable while still

30
00:02:34.056 --> 00:02:39.325
protecting state changes, reducing
accidental data exposure,

31
00:02:39.405 --> 00:02:45.747
preserving trustworthy logs, and preparing
the application for production

32
00:02:45.827 --> 00:02:46.900
conditions.
